{
  "abstract": "A formalization of A. Agazzi et al., Sharp higher integrability theory, Part I: the L^p-norm of the Beurling–Ahlfors transform (arXiv:2609.28311v2). With A z = a₊ z + a₋ z̄, the integrand L (A) = |a₊|² − |a₋|² for |a₊| + |a₋| ≤ 1 and 2|a₊| − 1 otherwise is quasiconvex on R^{2×2}: the integral over the torus of L (A + Dφ) − L (A) is nonnegative for every matrix A and every smooth (in fact every Lipschitz) periodic map φ (Theorem 1).\n\nHence (Corollary 2) the Burkholder integrands B_p are quasiconvex for every 1 < p < ∞, and the norm of the Beurling–Ahlfors transform on L^p(C) is p* − 1, p* = max (p, p/(p − 1)): Iwaniec's conjecture of 1982. Also proved: the Hessian case of Theorem 1, the uniqueness of the projection in Lemma 4, and Astala's sharp higher integrability of quasiconformal maps as a consequence of Corollary 2, with its sharpness. The cited results that the paper uses are proved too (Kinderlehrer–Stampacchia, Baernstein–Montgomery-Smith, Guerra, Lehto); the results it cites only for context, such as the earlier upper bounds for the norm and the earlier quasiconvexity results, are not formalized.",
